Scott asks to conclude from 3.11 and 3.12 that domains and approximable maps form a cartesian closed category, to name the terminal domain, and to say what sort of functor \((D_0\to D_1)\) is. `BasicInst4` shows there is at most one approximable map into the one-point domain, because its set of elements is a subsingleton, so any two maps agree on elements. `BasicInst5` shows there is a unique such map, the constant map at the default element, every map equalling it because there is at most one. `toUnit` names that default map as the map into the terminal one-point domain, and `toUnit_unique`, which is `example_323`, concludes that every approximable map into the one-point domain equals it. `homAdjunction` is the curry equivalence identifying approximable maps from a product into a domain with approximable maps from the first factor into the function space of the second factor and the codomain. That is the exponential adjunction. Binary products are not constructed here, no cartesian-closed category is assembled from products, the terminal object, and the exponential, and \((D_0\to D_1)\) is not described as a functor. **Theorem:** partial — the one-point domain is shown to be terminal and the exponential adjunction is the curry equivalence, but `example_323` is only uniqueness of the terminal map, and the functor question is unanswered. **Proof:** partial — terminal uniqueness is the subsingleton argument, and the adjunction is cited as curry rather than re-proved; products and functoriality of the function space are not proved.
